\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The grid operator}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The centered grid operator #{D} is not the Kähler–Dirac operator
#{\extD}.}

\p{The operators which \ref{fdip-00LB} builds from a flip and a sign
represent the basis vectors of #{\operatorname{Cl}(n,0)}. Those
vectors act by #{v\wedge+v\cdot}. The grid step keeps #{e_j},
and the represented generator in the same formula is
#{\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))}. The tensor product of flips and
signs is one representation of that action, and its Pauli arrays
are the example.}

\p{The value #{q_{\mathrm{sp}}(\ui\varepsilon_1)=-1} comes from
complexifying the coordinates. The Hermitian norm squared of the
same vector is #{1}.}
